// A dialect program that binds the dialect image (ui::WEB) and names `stderr`
// itself, including nothing. Bound from the image or parsed live, it emits the
// same C:
//   - its own `stderr` is the compiler's stream, `__madc_stdio_stream(2)` —
//     never a restored `extern void *stderr;` frozen from the image TU's own
//     stand-in (the stand-in is the compiler's declaration, not a record);
//   - the image bodies' `malloc` keeps the image's own declaration,
//     `void *malloc()` — never the system pack's `malloc(size_t)`, whose
//     typedef this program never declares (c2mir: "unknown type size_t").
//     The pack opens for `printf` (its prototype is adopted), which is what
//     put the pack's declaration ahead of the image's.
// Oracle: C11 7.21.1p3 — stderr is an expression of type FILE *; the C
// analogue (`#include <stdio.h>`, fputs to stderr, the printf line) behaves the same
// under gcc 13 and clang 18. The emitted C is the observable: both defects
// were bind-only (the packed binary), the run's output identical.
int main(void)
{
	long level = ui::WEB;
	fputs("user stderr line\n", stderr);
	printf("web above tui: %d\n", level > ui::TUI ? 1 : 0);
	return 0;
}
